1. IT Job Roles

Software Engineer: Designs, builds, and maintains software applications and systems.

Data Engineer: Builds pipelines to collect, clean, and organize large datasets for analysis.

DevOps Engineer: Automates and streamlines software deployment, system integration, and infrastructure management.

Cybersecurity Analyst: Protects networks, systems, and data from security breaches and cyber threats.

UI/UX Designer: Creates intuitive, user-friendly layouts and visual interfaces for digital products.

2. Product vs. Service Companies

Product Companies

Microsoft: Develops and sells standard software and hardware products like Windows and Xbox.

Adobe: Creates creative software packages like Photoshop and Premiere Pro for public distribution.

Salesforce: Builds and hosts cloud-based customer relationship management (CRM) software platform products.

Service Companies

Accenture: Provides custom IT consulting and digital transformation projects for enterprise clients.

Infosys: Offers outsourced software development and system integration services to businesses worldwide.

TCS (Tata Consultancy Services): Manages external IT infrastructure and custom enterprise software development for corporate clients.

3. Full Forms & Primary Uses

GitHub: Full form: GitHub (named after the Git version control system); used for hosting code repositories and managing collaborative software development.

Slack: Full form: Searchable Log of All Conversation and Knowledge; used for team communication, messaging, and project collaboration.

Jira: Full form: Derived from Gojira (Japanese for Godzilla); used for tracking project tasks, agile software development, and issue management.

IDE: Full form: Integrated Development Environment; used as a software application that provides coding tools, debugging, and text editing in one interface.

API: Full form: Application Programming Interface; used as a set of rules that allows different software applications to communicate and share data.

4. Programming Languages & Use Cases

Python: Popular for data analysis, artificial intelligence, and machine learning development.

JavaScript: Popular for building interactive elements and dynamic features on web pages.

Java: Popular for building large-scale, enterprise-level backend applications and Android mobile apps.

SQL: Popular for querying, updating, and managing data stored in relational databases.

C++: Popular for high-performance software development like 3D video games and operating systems.

5. Developer vs. Tester

A developer creates and writes code to build software features according to design requirements.

A tester evaluates the software to identify bugs, verify quality, and ensure the product works as expected.
